  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1202 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nA weak cool front was pushing into northwest IL at midday,\nassociated with a band of mid level clouds. HRRR and RAP \nforecast soundings indicate a prominent thermal ridge advecting \ninto central Illinois ahead of the front, with 850 mb \ntemperatures approaching 17-19 C. High temperatures reach the \nupper 80s, with a few locations topping out near 90 degrees \nunder mostly sunny skies. The dry and warm air mass aloft caps \nsurface- based parcel ascent across central Illinois, so we \nmaintain a dry forecast through tonight. \n\nThe surface boundary stalls across the forecast area on Sunday.\nHREF guidance shows a tight temperature gradient developing \nacross the local area. Highs range from the mid-to- upper 70s \nnorthwest of the Illinois River to the lower 90s south of \nInterstate 70. A persistent warm nose around 750 mb caps deep \nconvection along the front on Sunday, however weak isentropic \nascent generates saturation aloft, leading to mostly cloudy \nskies and scattered showers. HREF ensemble probabilities keep \nmeasurable precipitation chances between 20-40% north of I-70, \nwith QPF under a tenth of an inch.\n\nAn upper-level trough moves across the Northern Plains Monday, \npushing a surface front into the Upper Midwest. Decaying \nnocturnal convective remnants reach far western Illinois Monday \nmorning, but dry conditions are forecast across central and \nsoutheast Illinois. Highs to be seasonable in the upper 70s to\nmid 80s.\n\nThe pattern turns active Tuesday and Wednesday as a surface low\ntracks toward Hudson Bay, driving a cold front into northern or\ncentral Illinois where it stalls. ECMWF and GFS solutions show \n500 mb flow turning southwesterly, placing the region under \nfavorable upper-level jet dynamics. Moderate instability \ncombines with low-moderate deep layer shear to support \norganized convective structures. CIPS/CSU machine learning and\nAI guidance highlight a low potential for severe thunderstorms \nTuesday afternoon and Wednesday.  Convective training along the\nquasi- stationary boundary brings a risk for heavy rainfall \nTuesday into Wednesday. WPC Excessive Rainfall Outlooks \nmaintain a Marginal to Slight Risk across central Illinois. LREF\nensemble probabilities show a 40-60% chance for 24-hour QPF \nexceeding 1.0 inch north of a Beardstown to Bloomington line, \nwith 48-hour NBM mean precipitation totals exceeding 2.0 inches \nin far northwest counties near Galesburg. To the south of the \nactive storm clusters, a late season hot and humid airmass will \nprevail with highs in the low-mid 90s, and peak daily heat \nindices topping 100 degrees.\n\nThe baroclinic zone remains positioned over the region Thursday\nand Friday, though with less upper level support, PoPs are\nreduced to around 30% and severe/heavy rain risks appear quite\nlow. High temperatures maintain a north-to-south gradient, \nranging from the upper-70s near Galesburg to the lower 90s near \nLawrenceville.",
